{% extends '@nucleus/partials/particle.html.twig' %}

{% block particle %}
    {# This particle is only ever the container target that "menu breakpoint" JS
       (assets/common/application/menu/index.js) moves the real Menu particle's
       .g-toplevel markup into on mobile. It already lives inside the layout's
       Offcanvas section, which is its own sliding panel (#g-offcanvas, driven by
       assets/common/application/offcanvas/index.js and the header's
       data-offcanvas-toggle button) — NOT a Bootstrap .offcanvas component. Wrapping
       this in a real Bootstrap .offcanvas (as a prior pass did) leaves it
       visibility:hidden forever, since nothing ever adds Bootstrap's own .show class
       to it: the legacy outer panel slides into view but the menu items inside stay
       invisible. Keep this a plain container.

       TODO(bootstrap-conversion): revisit once the Offcanvas layout section itself
       (#g-offcanvas + assets/common/application/offcanvas/index.js's custom
       drag/slide JS) is migrated to real Bootstrap 5 Offcanvas markup. At that point
       this particle should render an actual `.offcanvas` component again, with the
       header's toggle button switched to data-bs-toggle="offcanvas" /
       data-bs-target, and the legacy offcanvas JS removed. Doing it now, before the
       rest of the template is fully Bootstrap, is what caused the double-offcanvas
       bug (empty-looking mobile menu) this comment replaced. #}
    <div id="g-mobilemenu-container" class="g-mobilemenu" data-g-menu-breakpoint="{{ genesis.config.get('styles.breakpoints.mobile-menu-breakpoint')|default('48rem') }}"></div>
{% endblock %}
